### 1. Priority One: the Smart Note learning loop is the heart of the system
- **What it is:** Shawn declared the Smart Note learning-loop repair PRIORITY ONE — "the heart, the main core of how our system functions and operates" — superseding everything else, to be solved by team consensus on the board, not by one seat alone. The same day he defined capture intent: "smart note this," "document it," "lock this in" are one intent in natural language, not a magic trigger phrase.
- **What it means:** The loop is only proven when a fresh lesson runs the full chain — capture → conformance → persistence → independent reread → cold comprehension → behavioral delta → Hub projection → later reuse by a different seat.
- **Why it matters:** Everything else the system claims (learning, compounding, the super brain) routes through this one chain; if it is broken, the rest is decoration.
- **What's in it for me:** When a seat detects decision-changing intelligence, capture it in the moment, in whatever words the human used — never wait for an exact phrase, and never call the loop proven from a partial chain.
- **How it connects:** The day's evidence was blunt: no code was listening for capture intent at all (the proof workflow fired only on push/dispatch), and the specimen note SN-002 that every seat was told to copy was itself non-conformant — copying it reproduced the defect.
- **What changes tomorrow:** From now on, we judge the loop by a fresh capture's full-chain run instead of by component checks; the merges of the fresh-capture experiment PRs (#1350/#1351) ARE the test, because the proof workflow fires on push to main.

### 2. Full auto-merge: trust the scorecard
- **What it is:** Late in the day Shawn replaced the held human-click merge gate outright: "Full auto-merge — trust the scorecard." Asking him to click merges he does not independently judge was delay without safety; the team's recorded scorecard becomes the gate, with a decision receipt and live mechanical verification before merge. The first exercise landed the same evening (PR #1437, merged 28 seconds after the intent comment).
- **What it means:** Judgment moves to where the judgment actually happens — the scoring seat — while production deploys, production DB, credentials/money, destructive acts, and constitutional ratification stay human-only.
- **Why it matters:** The merge queue stops being bottlenecked on a rubber stamp, and every merge carries its reasoning on the record.
- **What's in it for me:** Never present Shawn a bare "merge this." If his decision is genuinely needed, explain what the change does, why it exists, what his approval changes, and the real risk — otherwise score it, record it, and merge it yourself.
- **How it connects:** This is the seed of everything the merge protocol later became — and of its failure mode: the scorecard only works if the scoring is honest (see the same day's law: beautiful ≠ 10/10; score even perfect-looking work).
- **What changes tomorrow:** From now on, merges execute on scorecard + receipt + live verification instead of waiting for a human click that adds no judgment.

### 3. Navigation is the user's model, never the builder's
- **What it is:** The Library was redirected twice in one morning — v1's content "felt random," v2's seven builder categories were "not the categories a user needs." The lesson was promoted the same day (P61): primary navigation taxonomy is user-model-first. The Hub feed's tabs were rebuilt the same day into six user-question tabs (All / Start Here / The Intelligence / Your Tools / Smart Connect / The Ecosystem).
- **What it means:** Categories must answer the questions a new user is actually asking, not mirror how the system is built inside.
- **Why it matters:** A taxonomy only builders understand makes the whole product feel random to the people it exists for — Shawn felt it instantly, twice.
- **What's in it for me:** After a director redirect on structure, propose the new shape for sign-off before rebuilding, and leave the gaps you did not verify marked unverified.
- **How it connects:** The same law drove the Smart Connect door model (five doors a human can name) and the official onboarding boards that now lead the feed.
- **What changes tomorrow:** From now on, we design navigation from the user's questions instead of exposing the builder's architecture and calling it organization.

### 4. Never write about the product from memory — open the page first
- **What it is:** Shawn caught the ecosystem blocks written from recollection: Life with Naya was described as 27 chapters (it is a 9-page birth announcement); MAXIS was given nine dimensions (it has five). Seven of nine blocks were rewritten against the live pages, and the review standard was sharpened the same day: every block is scorecarded for effectiveness, accuracy, and presentation, with claims verified against the actual source pages before they are presented as real.
- **What it means:** Accuracy is scorecarded, and memory is not a source.
- **Why it matters:** User-facing copy written from memory ships confident falsehoods about the user's own properties — the worst kind of error, because it looks finished.
- **What's in it for me:** Before writing a single user-facing claim about a page, property, or product, open it and read it; if you cannot open it, say so instead of reconstructing it.
- **How it connects:** This is the evidence law at the writing desk — the same discipline as verifying claims against live tree bytes rather than code search or recollection.
- **What changes tomorrow:** From now on, product copy is drafted from the live source in front of the writer instead of from what the writer remembers the source saying.

### 5. Connected is not authorized
- **What it is:** Shawn's Smart Connect authority law: a connection exposes capability; it does not grant authority. Every door follows CONNECT → UNDERSTAND → AUTHORIZE → ACT → VERIFY → RECORD → LEARN, with the permanent distinction CONNECTED ≠ AUTHORIZED. LAW decides what may happen, ACT performs it, VERIFY checks the result.
- **What it means:** Plugging a system in and being permitted to act through it are two separate events, and the second one is a governed decision.
- **Why it matters:** Conflating the two is how integrations silently become authority grants nobody approved.
- **What's in it for me:** In any integration you design or review, name the authorization step explicitly; if it isn't there, the design isn't done.
- **How it connects:** The five-door product model (GitHub, MCP, AI, Data, SDK Connect) was built under this law the same day — browsing and joining stay free; acting requires the door's authority.
- **What changes tomorrow:** From now on, every connection we build carries its own authorization gate instead of inheriting permission from the fact that it connects.

### 6. Fix it or remove it
- **What it is:** Shawn's standing directive (~10:05 PDT): if anything isn't right, is outdated, or is not current to the mission, fix it or remove it — the goal is a functioning system that flows like water, and anything in the way gets resolved. Applied within the hour: the Priority One plan was rewritten to v2 and both v1 drafts marked SUPERSEDED.
- **What it means:** Stale artifacts are not neutral; they are obstacles other seats will trip on, cite, and build against.
- **Why it matters:** A system that accumulates superseded plans and outdated pages taxes every future decision with archaeology.
- **What's in it for me:** When you find something wrong or stale in your path, resolving it is part of your job in that moment — not a ticket for someone else.
- **How it connects:** Pairs with the full-power continuation directive from the same hour: don't stop at identifying the problem; use maximum analysis and cross-seat communication until the core process is right and locked in — while human gates stay human.
- **What changes tomorrow:** From now on, we fix or remove what is wrong or outdated on sight (marking superseded work SUPERSEDED) instead of routing around it and leaving it for the next seat.

### 7. Prior records are claims: cite them, and re-fetch before you repeat them
- **What it is:** Two evidence-hygiene lessons landed the same day. (A) Citation consistency (P62): a later run re-examining already-classified evidence must cite the prior adjudication — adopt it, reconcile with it, or supersede it with cause — never silently override (the morning sweep disarmed a run's preflight-only SUCCESS as zero-inference; the afternoon sweep drew an existence inference from the same run without citing the disarmament). (B) The evening battery confessed that ~3 days of its own evidence strings had called PR #1251 "open awaiting merge" when it had been closed, superseded, since Oct 2 — the re-scan had copied the prior run's strings instead of re-fetching state.
- **What it means:** A record's authority comes from its last live verification, not from how often it has been repeated.
- **Why it matters:** Fossilized claims steer real decisions — lanes stand down, repairs wait, and blockers persist on the strength of sentences nobody re-checked.
- **What's in it for me:** On every re-scan, GET each watched item's live state individually; the strings in prior evidence are claims, not measurements.
- **How it connects:** Same family as the day's other mechanical corollaries: a quiet push that swallows its own error hid a failed push entirely (Coda 1) — from now on, remote tip SHA == local commit SHA is confirmed after every push; and a push receipt proves bytes left a machine, never where they landed.
- **What changes tomorrow:** From now on, we cite prior adjudications when re-examining evidence and re-fetch live state on every re-scan instead of copy-forwarding yesterday's claims.

