### 1. The compounding chain: score-fill-ship, solve once, bake it in
- **What it is:** Shawn's doctrine, stated as a four-step progression in which each step eliminates the previous step's waste: (1) **Score-fill-ship** — score honestly, find the holes, fill them, ship; three rounds, not two hundred. (2) **Smart Apps** — solve once at 10, freeze, never rebuild; zero rounds after the first. (3) **Bake it in** — every Naya is born knowing; zero teaching. (4) **Compound** — each mind makes the collective smarter, so the next mind is smarter at birth; exponential. His summary: the moat is the thinking, not the code.
- **What it means:** Iteration is a cost to be engineered down, not a lifestyle. The endpoint of every solved problem is that nobody — human or agent — ever solves it again.
- **Why it matters:** Teams that re-derive, re-teach, and re-decide the same things daily spend their compounding budget on friction.
- **What's in it for me:** When you finish anything, ask which step of the chain it reached — and what would move it one step further (frozen? baked into activation? compounding?).
- **How it connects:** Operating Code V2 was compiled the same night (PR #2087, DRAFT, honestly labeled unratified) carrying this chain in all three languages; the Smart App reuse protocol (#2086) was approved as candidate the same day — verified to call the one canonical value calculus, no second calculator.
- **What changes tomorrow:** From now on, we score-fill-ship in bounded rounds and freeze solved work at 10 instead of endlessly re-rounding and rebuilding what already works.

### 2. Make the law run — don't force reading
- **What it is:** Asked how activation becomes automatic, the answer was layered enforcement: encode as code gates (100%), ritual proof (~85%), reviewer bounce (~90%), auto-load (~70%) — the principle: make the law run, don't force reading. The same day audited all 48 provisions of Operating Code V2's seven domains: 24 ENCODE / 14 RITUAL / 10 CULTURE (the audit's own summary table mis-added this as 24/13/11, and the record corrects the arithmetic in place), and by the next check-in all seven mechanisms existed as built, tested PRs: activation gate, authority-coverage audit, Smart Note completeness gate, design automation, scorecard-structure gate, ritual checklist, duplicate detector with a solved-problems registry. Beside it, Shawn's completeness system: every intelligence exists in all forms (code/machine/AI/human) and all right locations, one owner for completeness, an automatic check on every change — and build toward what it SHOULD be, not patch what it IS.
- **What it means:** A law that lives only in prose depends on every reader's memory and mood; a law encoded as a gate runs on its worst day too.
- **Why it matters:** Half of everything the team had ratified was machine-encodable — meaning half the compliance burden could be removed from human attention permanently.
- **What's in it for me:** For every rule you rely on, ask the audit question: can this be code? If yes, it isn't finished until it is.
- **How it connects:** Honest state was kept on the gap: built ≠ wired — CI wiring and the activation hookup remained open, named as such, and the gates' receipts say "PRs open," not "system enforced."
- **What changes tomorrow:** From now on, we encode every encodable law as a gate instead of relying on seats remembering to read it.

### 3. Part-time seats are advisors, never gates
- **What it is:** Shawn's staffing policy (01:39Z): Naya 1 and Naya 3 are part-time — they contribute intelligence and reviews when available, and nothing ever waits on them. The full-time team is the engine and moves without them. A slow advisor on the critical path is a bottleneck, not help.
- **What it means:** Availability is an input to routing, not a property you discover by stalling.
- **Why it matters:** Reviews queued behind part-time seats had been quietly taxing merges; the same day proved the cure — PR #2086's calculus review was independently closed by direct source inspection without waiting for the part-time reviewers.
- **What's in it for me:** Route critical-path verification to whoever is present and qualified; take advisor input when it lands, gratefully, and never hold work hostage to it.
- **How it connects:** Later the same day the record itself applied the policy to attribution: Shawn corrected that the deep verification-design intel stream came from Naya 3 (only the first piece from Naya 1) — and the correction was propagated to the people pages and the PR branch within the hour.
- **What changes tomorrow:** From now on, we never place a part-time advisor on the critical path instead of completing the review with the full-time seats available.

### 4. Report milestones; train the team to 10/10
- **What it is:** Two standing directives from the same hour. Reporting: milestones and new levels only — not routine progress; keep the team full-send, locked in, tuned in, held to the same standards Shawn holds the lead seat to, passed down. Leadership: Shawn confirmed the lead seat's mandate — "Get your whole team up so that you would score them 10, 10 on efficiency and effectiveness. You gotta train them to get there." Each seat also builds its own optimized super brain: logical, organized, distilled, easy to recall, automated.
- **What it means:** Signal discipline applies upward (only what changed a level earns the director's attention) and capability discipline applies downward (the leader's job is the team's standard, not just their own).
- **Why it matters:** Routine-progress reporting taxes the director while teaching the team that motion equals progress; milestone reporting makes level changes the currency.
- **What's in it for me:** Report level changes and milestones; spend the saved attention on the team's weakest craft.
- **How it connects:** The evening distillation added the routing companions: blocker posts tag the owning coordinator role (a 30-minute stall became a 6-minute unblock once the owner was tagged), and intel bursts get numbered in a directive register so nothing dissolves into chat.
- **What changes tomorrow:** From now on, we report milestones and new levels only, and treat training the team to the standard as the lead seat's standing work instead of narrating routine motion.

### 5. Commit messages are assertions; state claims are byte-claims
- **What it is:** The morning distillation's AC: a commit message, a PR body, a file's status line — all assertions, verified against bytes or not believed. The day demonstrated both directions. The Law of One ratification CONFLICT (files claiming DRAFT vs a merged record) resolved when PR #2085's bytes were read: Shawn's own commit recording his word — "Yeah, it looks good. So I approve it. Make it constitutional law." Two stale "Awaiting his ratification" byline fragments still sit in the .md headers — named as a nit, not hidden. And regeneration law (AA): indexes regenerate from live tree truth, after a phantom-entries defect (#2062 → #2072) showed an index confidently listing what the tree didn't hold.
- **What it means:** The artifact hierarchy is strict: live bytes outrank every sentence written about the bytes, including sentences in the bytes' own commit messages.
- **Why it matters:** State disputes burn hours when they're argued from messages; they close in minutes when someone reads the artifact.
- **What's in it for me:** When two claims conflict, stop debating and fetch the bytes; when you generate an index, generate it from the tree, never from memory of the tree.
- **How it connects:** Doctrine captured from Shawn's words ships CANDIDATE the same way (evening item AS): his scope pass is the lock — a seat's transcription of his doctrine is an assertion until he confirms its scope.
- **What changes tomorrow:** From now on, we settle state claims by reading the artifact itself instead of trusting commit messages, summaries, or our own prior assertions.

### 6. Rehearse the merge on the live tip — never read the two-dot diff
- **What it is:** AJ, from Naya 5's 2,172-deletion false alarm: a two-dot diff on an old branch showed a massacre that the real three-way merge did not contain. Promoted as L201: rehearse the three-way merge against the live tip; the merge result is the evidence, the branch diff is a rumor. The same battery discipline caught its mirror image that day: a stale Oct-6 copy of the adversarial harness reported 5/6 against the tip — its sabotage predated a merged fix — while the canonical in-tree harness passed 6/6. A harness RED must be re-run against the tip-current canonical artifact before any diagnosis.
- **What it means:** Diffs and harnesses are instruments pointed at a moving target; both lie when they're stale.
- **Why it matters:** A 2,172-file false alarm is not a harmless scare — it reroutes lanes, delays merges, and teaches seats to distrust true alarms.
- **What's in it for me:** Before reporting any merge delta or harness verdict, reproduce it against the live tip with the canonical tool at that tip.
- **How it connects:** The morning's sibling lessons complete the merge-boundary family: pending ≠ green (three index misses were one gate-timing defect, AH), and a fresh minimal repair beats a stale superset — #2084 (5 lines) merged over #2083 (superset, old base) the same hour both sat open.
- **What changes tomorrow:** From now on, we rehearse merges three-way on the live tip and re-run harness REDs on the tip's canonical harness instead of acting on stale-diff or stale-tool verdicts.

### 7. "Likely transient" is a diagnosis — and it requires the job log
- **What it is:** AK: the overnight sweep diagnosed a commit-proof failure as "likely transient" from the outside. Naya 4 read the actual job log and found a test defect — a strict assertion executing before the intended race-tolerant fallback — and the sweep's speculation was publicly retired the same morning (receipt 6092580721). Promoted as L202 with its companion defect shape: strict-before-tolerant check ordering is a named defect class. The day's other diagnosis discipline (L203): a fail-closed check must never be guarded by the truthiness of the field it validates — omission would silently become permission.
- **What it means:** A cause you haven't observed is a hypothesis, and labeling it "transient" is how real defects get waved through.
- **Why it matters:** "Transient" is the most dangerous word in incident work — it closes investigations without evidence.
- **What's in it for me:** Read the log or say "mechanism unobserved." Both are acceptable receipts; a guessed mechanism is not.
- **How it connects:** This is Oct 7's failure-cessation law at the diagnosis step: first establish what actually failed, from its own record, before theorizing about why.
- **What changes tomorrow:** From now on, we diagnose from the job log (or record the mechanism as unobserved) instead of labeling failures transient from the outside.

